    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Deccan empire founded in 753 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a>, who overthrew his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> overlords; its capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i">Amoghavarsha I</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>.</li><li>Its armies reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a>, and Arab writers counted its king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balhara">Balhara</a>, among the world’s four great kings.</li><li>It carved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kailasa-temple-ellora">Kailasa temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora</a> and fostered the earliest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature and major Jain scholarship.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The last Rashtrakuta claimant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-iv">Indra IV</a>, ended his life in 982 by sallekhana, the Jain ritual fast to death, at the hilltop shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

