    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dantidurga.jpg" alt="Dantidurga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rashtrakutas_Coins_25-07-23_15-44-48-445.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shraman revival, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rashtrakutas were a family of chiefs and officials in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, probably Kannada-speaking, who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> as feudatories in the early eighth century. Their earlier history is obscure, and several separate Rashtrakuta lineages are known from inscriptions. The founder of the imperial line, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a>, built up his power around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora</a> and the upper Godavari valley, and his inscriptions claim campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and Kosala; his record is carved in the Dashavatara cave at Ellora. The Samangad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate grant of 753 already shows him claiming paramount titles, and around that year he defeated the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtivarman-ii">Kirtivarman II</a>, ending Chalukya supremacy in the western Deccan. Dantidurga died c. 756 without a son and was succeeded by his uncle Krishna I, who completed the conquest of the remaining Chalukya lands, subdued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga dynasty</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi</a>. A grant of 812 praises Krishna I for a marvellous temple at Elapura, generally identified with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kailasa-temple-ellora">Kailasa temple</a> at Ellora, cut downwards into the basalt hillside as a single monolithic shrine. After the short reign of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-ii">Govinda II</a>, the throne passed c. 780 to Govinda’s brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha">Dhruva Dharavarsha</a>, who turned the kingdom into an empire contending for northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat.jpg" alt="Gulf of Khambhat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gujarat_Gulfs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rashtrakuta heartland lay on the black-soil plateau of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulses">pulses</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> were grown, and cotton textiles were among the region’s best-known products. Most people lived in villages that paid a share of the harvest to the king or to the holders of granted land, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate charters record gifts of villages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, temples and Jain monasteries. The empire controlled ports on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> and, through its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> branch, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a>, where Arab and Persian merchants traded. Arab writers, among them the merchant Sulaiman in 851 and al-Masudi, who visited western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> c. 916, called the Rashtrakuta king the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balhara">Balhara</a>, a corruption of the title Vallabharaja, and described him as friendly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and tolerant of Muslim communities in his ports. Religious life was plural: the kings were mostly devotees of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> enjoyed generous patronage. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i">Amoghavarsha I</a> the Jain teachers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinasena">Jinasena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunabhadra">Gunabhadra</a> composed the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adipurana-pampa">Adipurana</a></em>, and the mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahaviracharya">Mahaviracharya</a> wrote the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganitasarasangraha">Ganitasarasangraha</a></em> c. 850. Temples and Brahmin settlements served as centres of learning, such as the college at Salotgi recorded in 945. Merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> operated in the towns, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> were both used in inscriptions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha.jpg" alt="Dhruva Dharavarsha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha">Dhruva Dharavarsha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_(780_AD)_from_Naregal_of_Rashtrakuta_King_Dhruva_Dharavarsha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">E Hultzsch (Eugen Julius Theodor Hultzsch), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rashtrakuta power rested on large armies of infantry, cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> raised from the royal domain and from feudatories, and Arab authors were impressed by their size. The dynasty’s wars were dominated by the so-called tripartite struggle with the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty and the Pala Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>. Around 786–790 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha">Dhruva</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> and defeated both the Pratihara king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a> and the Pala king Dharmapala in the Ganga–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> doab. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iii">Govinda III</a> repeated the feat c. 800, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagabhata-ii">Nagabhata II</a>, while Dharmapala and his client <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chakrayudha">Chakrayudha</a> of Kannauj submitted; Rashtrakuta inscriptions claim that his horses drank from the Himalayan streams. Govinda III also broke a southern coalition of Pallava, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and Ganga rulers and took tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i">Amoghavarsha I</a> fought a long war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi</a>, winning at Vingavalli c. 830. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-iii">Indra III</a> marched north again and sacked Kannauj c. 916, humbling the Pratihara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahipala-i-pratihara">Mahipala I</a>. The last great commander, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a>, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butuga-ii">Butuga II</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-takkolam">Battle of Takkolam</a> in 949, where the Chola prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaditya">Rajaditya</a> was killed on his elephant, and occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tondaimandalam">Tondaimandalam</a> region around Kanchi for several years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/govinda-iii.jpg" alt="Govinda III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iii">Govinda III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inscription_of_Rashtrakuta_Emperor_Govinda_III_at_Veerabhadra_temple,_Mavali.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">B. Lewis Rice (Benjamin Lewis Rice, 1837-1927), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king took the imperial titles <em>maharajadhiraja</em>, <em>paramabhattaraka</em> and <em>parameshvara</em> and ruled with ministers, a crown prince and a court that issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate grants recording the royal genealogy and the transfer of land. Succession was frequently contested between brothers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha">Dhruva</a> displaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-ii">Govinda II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iii">Govinda III</a> had to defeat his brother Stambha, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iv">Govinda IV</a> was overthrown in a court revolt in 936. Under the king, the territory was divided into provinces called <em>rashtra</em> under a governor, districts called <em>vishaya</em>, smaller units called <em>bhukti</em>, and villages run by hereditary headmen with local assemblies. Much of the empire, however, was held by feudatories who paid tribute and supplied troops, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Gangas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vemulawada">Vemulavada</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silahara-dynasty">Silaharas</a> of the Konkan and, for a time, the Paramaras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. A collateral branch descended from Govinda III’s brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> governed southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> from c. 800. The capital moved several times: the early kings ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora</a> region, Govinda III from Mayurakhandi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i">Amoghavarsha I</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>, modern Malkhed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Kalaburagi</a> district, which remained the seat of government until 972. Amoghavarsha’s long reign from 814 to 878 was noted for internal peace, generous religious patronage and the king’s personal interest in literature.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kailasa-temple-ellora"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kailasa-temple-ellora.jpg" alt="Kailasa temple, Ellora" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kailasa-temple-ellora">Kailasa temple, Ellora</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kailash_temple_(Ellora_cave_no_15)_at_Verul.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For more than two centuries the Rashtrakutas were the leading power of peninsular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and one of the three great states of the subcontinent, and their campaigns reached both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> and the far south. Their reputation travelled abroad: Sulaiman’s account of 851 counted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balhara">Balhara</a> among the four great kings of the world, alongside the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, the emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Byzantine emperor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kailasa-temple-ellora">Kailasa temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora</a>, together with the Jain caves of the same site, is among the most ambitious works of rock-cut architecture in the world and part of the Ellora Caves World Heritage Site. The period was formative for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavirajamarga">Kavirajamarga</a></em>, a treatise on poetics attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i">Amoghavarsha I</a> or his court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, is the earliest surviving literary work in Kannada, and in the tenth century the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa">Pampa</a>, author of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramarjuna-vijaya">Vikramarjuna Vijaya</a></em> and a Jain <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adipurana-pampa">Adipurana</a></em> completed in 941, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ponna">Ponna</a> worked under the Rashtrakutas and their feudatories. Their patronage of Jain scholarship made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> learning. Politically, the empire’s feudatories became its heirs: the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silahara-dynasty">Silaharas</a> and the Paramaras all rose from its framework, and the Western Chalukya state largely took over its territory and administration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishna-iii.jpg" alt="Krishna III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_dated_Shaka_868_(c.944-45_AD)_of_Rashtrakuta_empire_King_Krishna_III.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jugaari cross, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire remained formidable under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a>, but its strength depended on his personal leadership and on loyal feudatories. After his death in 967 his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khottiga">Khottiga</a> faced the Paramara ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyaka">Siyaka II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, who invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a> in 972, an event recorded by the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhanapala-poet">Dhanapala</a>. The blow exposed the weakness of the throne. In 973 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tailapa-ii">Tailapa II</a>, a feudatory based in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> region who claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>, overthrew Khottiga’s nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karka-ii">Karka II</a> and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a>, soon making himself master of most of the old Rashtrakuta lands. Resistance continued in the south, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marasimha-ii">Marasimha II</a> and his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavundaraya">Chavundaraya</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-iv">Indra IV</a>, a grandson of Krishna III, as Rashtrakuta claimant. The cause collapsed after Marasimha’s death, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> IV died in 982 at the Jain centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a> by the ritual fast of <em>sallekhana</em>, ending the imperial line. Minor Rashtrakuta families persisted as local chiefs, and some later dynasties of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> claimed descent from them, though these links are uncertain. The Western Chalukyas inherited the empire’s position in the Deccan, continuing its rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> for another two centuries.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Rashtrakuta dynasty made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> the centre of Indian power for two centuries, sending armies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a>, earning a place among the world’s four great kings in Arab accounts, carving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kailasa-temple-ellora">Kailasa temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora</a> and sponsoring the first flowering of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature.</em></p>

